     The window menu's SSeeaarrcchh entry opens the same dialog: a FFiinndd
     pattern, a RReeppllaaccee text, and three verbs.  FFiinndd goes to the
     next match, wrapping past the end of the buffer, and closes
     the dialog so the hit can be seen; a match that does not ex-
     ist leaves the dialog up saying so.  RReeppllaaccee rewrites the
     match under the cursor and steps to the next - so the first
     RReeppllaaccee after opening the dialog only finds a match, and the
     second changes it.  AAllll replaces every match from the cursor
     to the END of the buffer (not the whole file) and reports
     the count.  Matching is case-sensitive and a match never
     spans a newline; a replacement that would push a line past
     its stored length is refused rather than truncated.  EESSCC rr
     does what RReeppllaaccee does but without the dialog covering the
     text, which is the way to work through a buffer match by
     match.  Function keys, laid out like the Norton Commander
     editor's key bar amended (F1 = mark and F4 = replace are
     swapped from NC - NC's F1, help, is the HELP key here - F3
     stays Open, and F9 pastes):

                FF11          mark: start a keyboard block at the cursor; moving
