     A block from FF11 works with mouse selections' verbs inter-
     changeably: F5/F6/F8 and the menu act on whichever selection
     exists.  The remaining shifted and control function keys
     type mmee-only chords (macros, buffers, window sizes) that
     zzeeddiitt ignores; the full byte map is the zztteerrmm manual page's
     table.  New is also in the window menu.  Printable charac-
     ters and TAB insert at the cursor.  Tabs are stored liter-
     ally and displayed at 8-column stops.  RETURN splits the
     line at the cursor.

     The mouse places the cursor with a left click, and a left
     drag selects text; on release the selection becomes the
     PRIMARY selection, which a middle click pastes in any GUI
     window (at the click point).  Cut and Copy instead use the
     clipboard, a separate store, so a clipboard paste and a mid-
     dle-click paste can carry different text.

     The window menu offers New, Open, Save, Cut, Copy, Paste,
     Help and Search above the standard window entries.  Menu
     Save always shows the file-name dialog, prefilled with the
     current name; FF22 saves silently under the current name.  In
     dialogs, TAB cycles among text fields, RETURN fires the
     default button and ESC cancels.

